      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L01-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "1.4: Matching Exponent Expressions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Exponent rules help us keep track of a base’s repeated factors. Negative exponents help us keep track of repeated factors that are the reciprocal of the base. We can define a number to the power of 0 to have a value of 1. These rules can…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l01.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L01-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "1.4: Matching Exponent Expressions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\begin{align}b^m \\boldcdot b^n &= b^{m+n} \\\\ \\left(b^m\\right)^n &= b^{m \\boldcdot n} \\\\ \\frac{b^m}{b^n} &= b^{m-n} \\\\ b^{\\text-n} &= \\frac{1}{b^n} \\\\ b^0 &= 1 \\\\ a^n \\boldcdot b^n &= (a \\boldcdot b)^n \\end{align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l01.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L01-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "1.4: Matching Exponent Expressions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here, the base \\(b\\) can be any positive number, and the exponents \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) can be any integer.",
